1. Parth — Database & Backend Integration
"Hi everyone, I’m Parth, and I handled the database design and backend integration for our project.
To get our data storage up and running, we used our schema.sql script to set up the employeeData database, and I wired up our secure JDBC connection properties. On the development side, I built out the MySQLEmployeeRepository class. This handles all the heavy lifting for our core database operations—like inserting new workers, deleting records, updating details, and executing secure, parameterized searches by ID, name, or SSN using PreparedStatement interfaces to keep everything safe and running smoothly.
Now, Snigdha will walk you through our core object models."
2. Snigdha — Model Classes & Architecture
"Hey everyone, I’m Snigdha. I built out our core domain model and object-oriented architecture to keep our code clean and closely mimic real life.
We used a base Person class and a Payable interface, which fed into our main Employee class to track IDs, salaries, departments, and complete pay histories using our PayrollRecord class. We also used type-safe JobTitle and Division enums to keep our data structured and error-free.
Ultimately, this setup let our Java objects map seamlessly straight to our backend database.
With that architecture set, I'll hand things over to Maia!"
3. Maia — Salary Modifications & Reports
"Hello everyone, I’m Maia, and I built out the advanced reporting and salary manipulation logic inside our repository layer.
Once we had our standard CRUD operations in place, we needed smart tools to handle real-world workforce updates and analytics. So, I implemented methods that allow managers to give percentage-based salary raises, update salaries within specific ranges, and pull up detailed chronological payroll histories. I also wrote aggregate SQL queries that instantly calculate total payroll expenditures broken down systematically by individual job titles and corporate divisions.
From here, I'll pass it over to Iyana to showcase our user interface."
4. Iyana — JavaFX User Interface & Video Demo
"Hi everyone, I’m Iyana, and I developed the front-end graphical user interface using JavaFX.
Our goal was to create a clean, responsive desktop application where users can easily navigate dedicated screens to insert, update, delete, and search employee records, as well as view financial reports. Every single button and form action is wired directly into our backend repository methods, meaning every user interaction instantly translates into safe database transactions with clear feedback. I also put together and edited our team's final video demonstration.
Now, Jalen will cover our testing, quality assurance, and documentation phase."
5. Jalen — Testing, QA & Final Documentation
"Hi everyone, I’m Jalen, and I oversaw the testing, quality assurance, and project documentation.
Before we wrapped things up, we ran thorough integration and system tests to make sure our JavaFX interface, backend repository, and database all communicated seamlessly across our local machines. We walked through our complete test suite, tracked our pass and fail criteria, and verified that every single project requirement was fully met. Working closely with Jaay on final reviews, we made sure everything was documented cleanly in our SWDD report.
Thank you all for watching, and we'd love to open the floor to any questions!"
6. Jaay — Project Coordination & Final Packaging
"And a quick word from me, Jaay—I handled overall project coordination, making sure all our individual modules integrated smoothly together. I also helped assemble our SWDD report, converted our files to PDF, and packaged everything cleanly into our final ZIP file for submission. Thank you all!"
